- How would you extend to count the exact composition of nodes chosen for the final K-set?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: per-block setup cost while selecting up to k blocks, non-overlapping; tests ability to derive a compact DP with prefix sums and a best-so-far trick; includes reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays with a per-block cost
- Prefix sums to compute block values quickly
- Best-so-far technique to achieve O(nk) time
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKBlocksWithCost(A, k, cost) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => new Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  const bestVal = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  bestVal[0] = 0; // dp[0][0] - P[0]

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const candidate = P[i] - cost + bestVal[t-1];
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], candidate);
    }
    // update bestVal for next i
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const val = dp[i][t-1] - P[i];
      if (val > bestVal[t]) bestVal[t] = val;
    }
  }
  return { dp, P };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP setup for segment selection with non-adjacency and reconstruction, a common beginner DP pattern with a twist.

## Key Concepts
- DP over index i and blocks t
- Enforce non-adjacency via s-2 reference
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints
- Time/space trade-offs; possible optimization to O(nk) time and O(n) space per k

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i + 1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i - 1][t]);
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
          const sum = P[i] - P[s - 1];
          const prev = (s >= 2) ? dp[s - 2][t - 1] : (t - 1 === 0 ? 0 : -Infinity);
          const cand = sum + prev;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            take[i][t] = s;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back using 'take' from (n, bestT)
  return { maxSum: dp[n][0], blocks: [] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend Kadane-like DP to k non-overlapping segments with per-element penalties and reconstruction of exact endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP for k disjoint subarrays with penalties
- Local/global state separation for endings vs global maxima
- Backpointer reconstruction to recover endpoints
- Time complexity O(nk), space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, p) {
  const n = A.length;
  const negInf = -1e18;
  const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  const global = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(negInf));
  global[0][0] = 0;
  // backpointers omitted for brevity in this snippet
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const val = A[i - 1] - p;
      local[i][t] = Math.max(local[i - 1][t] + val, global[i - 1][t - 1] + val);
      global[i][t] = Math.max(global[i - 1][t], local[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return { maxValue: global[n][k], endpoints: [] };
}
```

